rut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>","key":"NOkAx9za3M"},{"type":"text","value":"; that is, the case when flow has no effect on mass flux.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"uZj9hzFT36"}],"key":"yFNcJLBIyn"}],"visibility":"show","key":"vqtUbXqEUz"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{"collapsed":false,"editable":true,"jupyter":{"outputs_hidden":false},"slideshow":{"slide_type":""},"tags":[]},"children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sherwood number calculator","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"w4ZdE2RyxL"}],"identifier":"sherwood-number-calculator","label":"Sherwood number calculator","html_id":"sherwood-number-calculator","implicit":true,"key":"GB62kw6cix"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As you can see in the plot above, under some conditions mass transport is greatly increased by fluid flow.\nBut, under other conditions, the increase is minimal.\nWhich applies to a given situation?","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"CZ1GzhhxPX"}],"key":"zu8Z0VogoA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The text panel below enables you to enter the particle size, density and surface solute concentration, and the fluid viscosity, density and solute concentration.\nThe worksheet will then calculate for you the corresponding ","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"UxljcaJIML"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\mathcal{Re}","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{Re}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\">R</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span></span></span></span></span>","key":"x5uSQXTeTV"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"GZxcuPh3un"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\mathcal{Sh}","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{Sh}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.075em;\">S</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span></span></span></span></span>","key":"N5bm9RBv75"},{"type":"text","value":" and mass transport rates.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"kcdp0UTfQD"}],"key":"dMNVZCiRwq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In many environmental problems, important issues involve the amount of mass transport per unit mass of particle.\nFor example, a pollution problem may involve knowing whether a given mass of pollutant divided into many small particles has a different environmental effect from the same mass in a few larger particles.\nIn the example of nutrient uptake by phytoplankton, an ecological question might revolve around whether a given biomass of small cells is more effective at competing for nutrients than an equal biomass of large cells.\nThe calculator enables you to address those questions by calculating the mass transport per unit mass of particle.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"RE2Re6ntim"}],"key":"Vga3elSSaZ"}],"visibility":"show","key":"gB6Xh7YdXk"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","data":{"editable":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"slideshow":{"slide_type":""},"tags":[]},"children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"mu7=widgets.FloatText(value=1.376e-3,width=20,description = r\"$\\mu~~ (\\frac{N s}{m^2})$\")\nrho_water=widgets.FloatText(value=1028.,description = r\"$\\rho~~ (\\frac{kg}{m^3})$\")\nrho_sphere=widgets.FloatText(value=1105.,description = r\"$\\rho_{sphere}~~ (\\frac{kg}{m^3})$\")\nC_s=widgets.FloatText(value=0.,description = r\"$C_s$\")\nC_w=widgets.FloatText(value=1.,description = r\"$C_w$\")\nD7=widgets.FloatText(value=5.e-6,description = r\"$D~~ (m)$\")\nK7=widgets.FloatText(value=0.001,description = r\"$K~~ (\\frac{m^2}{s})$\")\n\nui1e = widgets.VBox([mu7,rho_water,C_w])\nui2e = widgets.VBox([rho_sphere,D7,C_s])\nui3e = widgets.HBox([ui1e, ui2e])\n\ndef Sh7(rho_water,rho_sphere,D7,mu7,K7,C_w,C_s):\n    rr=D7/2.\n    vol=float(4./3.*pi*rr**3)\n    excess_mass=-(rho_sphere-rho_water)*vol # Keep the same sign convention as \n                                            # Het_labX.sws -- positive z is up\n    g=9.81\n    ff=g*excess_mass\n    if rho_water==rho_sphere:\n        print('External force is zero...')\n        U_s=0.\n    else:\n        U_s=U_sphere(rr,ff,rho_water,mu7)\n    print('Volume = {:.4g}'.format(vol))\n    print('Excess mass = {:.4g}'.format(excess_mass))\n    print('Gravity/buoyancy force = {:.4g}'.format(g*excess_mass))\n    print('Reynolds number: Re = {:.4g}'.format(2.*rr*abs(U_s)*rho_water/mu7))\n    print('U = {:.4g}'.format(U_s))\n\n    Flux_diff= float(4 * pi * K7 * rr * (C_w-C_s))\n    #print '\\nFlux_diff=',Flux_diff\n    Sh = Sherwood(rr,K7,rho_water,mu7,abs(U_s))\n    Flux_adv=float(Sh*Flux_diff)\n    print('\\nSherwood number: Sh = {}'.format(Sh))\n    print('Diffusive transport: Q_diff = {}'.format(Flux_diff))\n    print('Advective transport: Q_adv = {}'.format(Flux_adv))\n    \n    print('\\nAdvective transport per unit mass: Q_adv/Excess mass = {}'.format(Flux_adv/abs(excess_mass)))   \n    \n    \nout7 = widgets.interactive_output(Sh7,{'rho_water':rho_water,'rho_sphere':rho_sphere,\n                                      'D7':D7,'mu7':mu7,'K7':K7,'C_s':C_s,'C_w':C_w})\ndisplay(ui3e,out7)","visibility":"show","key":"KlEFCSnKDE"},{"type":"outputs","id":"bXVxh0KOPayvSSHBOC6zT","children":[],"visibility":"show","key":"soZWUqG9hz"}],"visibility":"hide","key":"HYNUzQ7xWY"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{"editable":true,"slideshow":{"slide_type":""},"tags":[]},"children":[{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"_lift","remove-input":true,"source":{"label":"rs3_1"},"key":"lFLZTofbhI"},{"type":"image","placeholder":true,"url":"/build/RS3_1-05bd28854914111ba1d803ba6af9c9cf.png","key":"QytGCJYdpc","urlSource":"Images/RS3_1.png"}],"enumerator":"2","source":{"label":"rs3_1"},"key":"Sm8MAQFoG7"}],"visibility":"show","key":"XIoNUN3Vvc"}],"key":"scGhB0632V"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"📖️ Mass flux in and out of moving organisms","url":"/rs3context","group":"Biomechanics"},"next":{"title":"🏞️ Size and swimming in early-stage marine invertebrate larvae","url":"/chimeraswim","group":"Biomechanics"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3001"}